# Target C — C81 one-edge chain spectral stability frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_chain_face_spectral_stability_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_chain_face_spectral_stability_2026-08-31.md).

C80 gives an exact chain diameter bar `h_0` whose train-angle Hessian is `-D I` on
the `D-1` dimensional chain-bending space

`W_P={v supported on the D chain tracks, sum_P v=0}`.

C81 proves this negative spectrum is stable if one diameter edge keeps almost all beta
mass.

For every diameter bar in a pure rhombic train system,

`||H_h||_op <= D+T`,

where `T` is the number of train tracks.

If a normalized KKT stress satisfies

`beta_h0 >=1-delta`,

then its stress-projected train Hessian

`Q_s=sum_h beta_h H_h`

obeys on `W_P`

`Q_s[v,v] <= -[D-delta(2D+T)] ||v||^2`.

Thus, if

`delta < D/(2D+T)`,

then

`nu_-(Q_s)>=D-1`.

In the pure-rhombic regime `T=O(D)`, one-edge concentration

`beta_h0=1-o(1)`

forces `D-1` negative eigenvalues with gap `(1-o(1))D`.

## Exact missing stress-side lemma

C66 only gives star-center concentration

`sum_(h incident z) beta_h=1-o(1)`.

C79 does not force one leaf to carry almost all of that mass. Therefore the next
stress-side target is now explicit:

- prove one `z`-star edge has beta `1-o(1)`; or
- prove a multi-leaf star necessarily pays a response/radial/reciprocal defect that
  rules out `D epsilon->0`.

C81 also does not remove the Target B direction-dependent secondary-stress loophole;
it quantifies the burden those residual blockers must overcome.